: Two choices differ from the first plan. A built entry is capped at a
  tenth of a page (812 bytes), the limit nbtsort.c's <code>_bt_load</code>
  puts on posting lists, not at <code>BarkMaxItemSize</code>: entries of a
  third of a page leave room for only two beside the high key, and the
  first version, cut at <code>BarkMaxItemSize</code>, built ks in 11041
  pages, more than btree. And <code>bark_form_posting</code> built its sbm
  one locator at a time, re-walking the chunk list from its head each time,
  which is quadratic on a scattered set. The build probes POSTING on every
  LIST-sized run (column 3). It now builds the sbm in one pass with
  <code>sbm_create_from_array</code>, which retail insert's general
  coalesce path also gets. A parallel build writes the same tree. Retail
  insert can still grow a built entry up to <code>BarkMaxItemSize</code>.

'''Recommendation, in order.'''

# '''Drop the cardinality hint from <code>sbm_serialize</code>.''' One
  commit in <code>sbm.c</code>/<code>sbm.h</code>. Write 0 in bytes 8-15, or
  write the cached value only when it is already valid, and document the
  field as reserved. Either way the wire format does not change, so
  <code>SBM_WIRE_VERSION</code> stays. The <code>test_sbm</code>
  round-trip tests cover it, since nothing reads the field. Cuts insert
  time for POSTING appends by 21% (kc) to 31% (''one''). Changes no page or
  WAL byte, and touches no file other work is changing.
# '''Batch POSTING decode.''' One commit: <code>bark_posting_get_tids</code>
  decodes through <code>sbm_scan</code>, about 15 lines, which is the change
  measured above. Test: the POSTING differential tests in
  <code>bark.sql</code> already compare every scan mode with a sequential
  scan. Makes the POSTING bitmap node 6-10x faster and index-only scans
  35-40% faster. The page-at-a-time scan position also reads members
  through <code>bark_entry_get_tids</code>, so it gets the speedup without
  changes.
# '''Form LIST/POSTING at CREATE INDEX''' (option f), done. One commit in
  <code>barksort.c</code>, with a size test: build vs retail page counts
  within a factor, <code>bark_index_check</code> clean, index results equal
  to a sequential scan. Leaves stay packed to fillfactor, as now. The
  build's <code>bark_form_hikey</code> and <code>bark_form_downlink</code>
  truncate with <code>index_truncate_tuple</code>, which copies the whole
  tuple, body included, when the index has no INCLUDE columns. Once the
  build emits LIST and POSTING entries, pivots must be formed from the
  entry stripped to its key, as <code>bark_strip_to_key</code> does for
  insert-side splits. Suffix truncation's build path, which forms the pivot
  from lastleft and firstright, needs the same.
# '''The page-fill merge pass''' (option c). One commit, after scans keep a
  page-at-a-time position. Today a scan resumes by page offset, and a pass
  that rewrites a page moves offsets just as an insert does. A page-at-a-time
  scan copies a page's matches when it reads the page, as
  <code>_bt_readpage</code> does, and that copy is what makes nbtree's
  in-place dedup pass safe for scans. The pass runs under the leaf's exclusive
  lock, at the point where <code>bark_insert</code> would call
  <code>bark_split</code>. That is the slot nbtree uses
  (<code>_bt_delete_or_dedup_one_page</code>, just before
  <code>_bt_split</code>). It replaces <code>bark_coalesce_list</code>, leaves
  the split protocol alone, and writes its page rewrite as one generic record.
  VACUUM cycle IDs are not affected, because the pass never moves an entry to
  another page. If leaf prefix compression lands first, the pass reads items
  through its leaf-item accessor and rebuilds the page as a split does,
  re-encoding against the page's anchor. Tests: bark.sql differential per-key
  counts and order after mixed-order inserts into a few hot keys, amcheck
  clean, and a WAL-volume check on ks against the per-insert coalesce.
  Expected: ks WAL 11.6 GB down to about 2-3 GB, and insert time down to near
  SINGLE-only, measured on a quiet host.
# '''A BARK WAL rmgr''' (option e), as a series once the concurrency, VACUUM
  and split work has merged. The first commit is done: the "Bark" resource
  manager with <code>rm_mask</code>, and records for VACUUM (replayed under a
  cleanup lock), page deletion, freeing an overflow page and page reuse, whose
  replay resolves the hot-standby conflict that page recycling needs
  (<code>027_stream_regress</code> passes under
  <code>wal_consistency_checking=all</code>; see "WAL" in the bark README).
  The second adds the insert records (<code>INSERT_LEAF</code>,
  <code>INSERT_UPPER</code>, and <code>OVERWRITE</code> for a grown LIST or
  POSTING entry). Release build, 1M retail inserts, three rounds:
  unique keys 2.7-2.9 s to 1.27 s and 167 to 151 MB of WAL; 1000 clustered
  keys (POSTING) 3.3-3.7 s to 1.74 s, but 481 to 705 MB; 1000 scattered keys
  (LIST) 6.3-6.9 s to 2.44 s and 1782 to 1282 MB. <code>OVERWRITE</code> logs
  the whole new entry, which a LIST grows up to the 2712-byte item limit, and
  an sbm add changes fewer bytes than the entry holds. The third commit logs
  only the added locator (<code>ADD_TID</code>, as nbtree's
  <code>INSERT_POST</code>; redo re-forms the entry): POSTING appends 1.74 to
  1.24 s and 705 to 121 MB (481 MB with generic WAL), LIST appends 2.44 to
  1.42 s and 1282 to 129 MB (1782 MB with generic WAL). The fourth logs
  splits, new roots and an empty index's first root (<code>SPLIT</code>,
  <code>NEWROOT</code>, <code>CREATE_ROOT</code>): each split half is logged
  as its packed entries and rebuilt in redo, with no page image. The fifth
  logs overflow chains (<code>OVERFLOW</code>), the last generic WAL: every
  change BARK makes after CREATE INDEX is now in a Bark record.

Not recommended: a per-key posting tree (b) and a pending list (d), for the
reasons above. If C-DELETE or retail delete later needs to find one
(key, locator) pair, make the locator a tie-breaking key attribute in
pivots, as nbtree did in version 4, rather than adding a second tree.

=== C-STATS: statistics and cost ===

BARK feeds the planner the same statistics contract as nbtree:
<code>amcostestimate</code> produces comparable correlation, per-tuple, and
startup costs; <code>ANALYZE</code> sees index expressions the same way; the
planner's ordered-path generation (see the sweep below) treats a BARK scalar
index as interchangeable with a B-tree for costing.

=== The capability sweep ===

There are currently '''30''' <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code> references in the tree.
They fall into three groups; only the middle group is BARK work.

# '''Must stay nbtree (policy).''' Catalog and TOAST index creation
  (<code>catalog/toasting.c</code>), <code>pg_amcheck</code>
  (<code>src/bin/pg_amcheck</code>), and the opclass/opfamily lookup helpers
  that answer "what is the btree opclass for this type"
  (<code>typcache.c</code>, <code>lsyscache.c</code>, <code>typecmds.c</code>,
  <code>opclasscmds.c</code>, <code>amvalidate.c</code>,
  <code>nbtvalidate.c</code>, <code>parse_clause.c</code>). These legitimately
  mean the B-tree operator family, not "the only ordered AM," and are left
  alone.
# '''The sweep target: "btree is the only thing that can produce ordered
  paths / be sorted on / infer an ON CONFLICT arbiter."''' The planner and
  executor sites -- <code>optimizer/path/indxpath.c</code>,
  <code>optimizer/util/plancat.c</code>,
  <code>utils/sort/tuplesortvariants.c</code> (the
  <code>Assert(relam == BTREE_AM_OID)</code>), <code>commands/repack.c</code>,
  <code>utils/adt/ri_triggers.c</code>, <code>commands/tablecmds.c</code> --
  are rewritten to ask an AM capability ("can produce ordered scans",
  "supports this sort") rather than to compare against
  <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code>. This is the largest mechanical part of the series.
# '''Ambiguous, decided case by case''' (<code>access/index/amapi.c</code>'s
  index-AM validation): keep the btree-specific validation for btree, add
  BARK's own.

KNN ordering (<code>amcanorderbyop</code>) is a reserved field in BARK's
<code>IndexAmRoutine</code> from the first commit but is not implemented until
phase P07, so the sweep only has to make ordered ''sort'' paths capability-
driven now, not ordered ''operator'' paths.

== Roadmap ==

Reconstructed phase plan. Each entry is one or a few bisectable commits; every
commit builds under <code>-Werror --enable-cassert</code> and passes
<code>make check</code> on its own (regenerate the initdb template --
<code>trash tmp_install testrun; meson test --suite setup</code> -- before the
regress step so a stale template cannot produce a false diff). Contracts above
are cited by name in each phase's commit messages.

'''Done'''

* '''0001''' -- this design document (the contract of record).
* '''sbm ADT''' -- vendored sparse bitmap set, PG-native, standalone-tested.
  ''(committed)''
* '''test_sbm''' -- the sbm regression + differential-test module, plus the
  sbm-vs-Bitmapset-vs-TIDBitmap benchmark. ''(committed)''

'''F-series -- foundations (make a BARK peer of nbtree structurally)'''

* '''F01''' -- vendor the substrate. ''(done as the sbm ADT above.)''
* '''F02''' -- cherry-pick / rebase the C-LOCATOR contract
  (<code>amlocator.h</code>, <code>amcanlocators</code>,
  <code>locator_capability</code>, the <code>DefineIndex</code> check) from
  <code>tepid</code>.
* '''F03''' -- cherry-pick the recheck-hint bit
  (<code>ItemPointerRecheckHintFlag</code>) from <code>tepid</code>.
* '''F04''' -- the capability sweep, group 2: make ordered-''sort'' path
  generation, <code>tuplesort</code> variants, <code>repack</code>, and the RI
  trigger ask an AM capability instead of comparing to
  <code>BTREE_AM_OID</code>. No BARK code yet; nbtree still answers "yes" to
  every capability, so this is behavior-preserving.
* '''F05''' -- register the empty <code>bark</code> AM: catalog rows,
  <code>IndexAmRoutine</code> skeleton (all capability flags declared,
  including reserved <code>amcanorderbyop</code>), <code>amvalidate</code>,
  build/scan hooks that error "not implemented". <code>CREATE INDEX USING
  bark</code> is accepted and validated but cannot yet be built.
* '''F06''' -- the on-disk page format: meta/root/leaf/internal page layout,
  the C-KEYSHAPE entry encoding (single / sorted-list / posting-set), and the
  reserved delete-marked subtype for C-DELETE. Documented as a companion
  <code>README</code> under the AM directory.

'''A-series -- the working access method'''

* '''A01-A03''' -- insert and split (single-locator shape first): leaf insert,
  page split, high-key / right-link maintenance in the Lehman & Yao tradition
  nbtree uses.
* '''A04-A06''' -- search and ordered scan; the C-MVCC leaf-key recheck and
  index-only-scan support; SSI predicate locking at nbtree granularity.
* '''A07-A09''' -- uniqueness, multicolumn keys, INCLUDE columns.
* '''A10-A12''' -- the sorted-duplicates and posting-set shapes (C-KEYSHAPE),
  sbm-backed for clustered value sets; BitmapAnd/BitmapOr via sbm set-algebra.
* '''A13-A15''' -- deletion, VACUUM, page recycling; then C-DELETE
  delete-marking (<code>amdeletemark</code>/<code>amundomark</code>) once the
  table is UNDO-backed. Page recycling follows nbtree: a deleted page keeps
  its sibling links and records a safexid, and is reused only once
  <code>GlobalVisCheckRemovableFullXid</code> says no snapshot can still hold
  a link to it (the README's "Page deletion and recycling"). Bottom-up
  deletion, as nbtree's: an <code>indexUnchanged</code> insert that would
  split a leaf first asks the table AM which of the leaf's versions are dead
  and deletes their entries (<code>XLOG_BARK_DELETE</code>; the README's
  "Bottom-up deletion").
* '''A16-A18''' -- parallel build, parallel scan, and the amcheck contract for
  BARK.

'''P-series -- parity and beyond'''

* '''P01-P03''' -- performance parity with nbtree on the scalar workload
  (build throughput, scan latency, space) using the benchmark harness pattern
  from test_sbm; A/B against <code>master</code>'s nbtree.
* '''P04-P06''' -- arbitrary-size keys/values beyond the nbtree page cap; the
  C-STATS cost model tuned to the new shapes.
* '''P07''' -- KNN / <code>amcanorderbyop</code>: implement ordered-''operator''
  scans and extend the capability sweep to ordered-operator path generation.
* '''Leaf prefix compression''' -- the <code>prefix_compression</code>
  reloption: a leaf flagged <code>BARK_PREFIX</code> holds a PREFIX item, and
  its entries store the first key column front-coded against it (a shared
  count, then the rest), each entry recording its own share, so the prefix is
  chosen only when a leaf is written whole (CREATE INDEX, either half of a
  split) and never forces a rewrite. <code>BarkPageGetItem</code> decodes;
  coding is catalog-free, so ADD_TID redo repeats it. Format in the README's
  "Leaf prefix compression". nbtree has nothing comparable. ''(done)''

'''H/D-series -- nbtree parity that remains'''

* '''H1''' -- order duplicates by heap TID (nbtree's heapkeyspace): pivots
  keep a heap TID when both sides are equal on every key column, inserts
  descend by (key, TID), an insert inside a full LIST or POSTING swaps with
  its highest TID. Requires REINDEX of indexes built before it.
* '''D1''' -- bottom-up index deletion on the executor's
  <code>indexUnchanged</code> hint, with its own WAL record and
  recovery-conflict resolution.
* '''K2''' -- dead-tuple hints from scans and simple deletion before a split.
  After <code>amgetbatch</code> (CF 4351) is in core and BARK adopts it, since
  the hint goes through <code>amkillitemsbatch</code>.

'''M-series -- documents (multikey)'''

These make BARK an index that a document store such as DocumentDB can use in
place of an inverted AM, without changing the scalar design above.

* '''M1''' -- multikey keys: an opclass support procedure that extracts N
  keys from one column value, called by insert and build; one row's sorted
  keys are inserted in one pass that reuses the descent. A scan returns a row
  that matches through several keys once. The meta page records whether the
  index has multikey rows. Needs H1, so that (key, TID) names exactly one
  entry member.
* '''M2''' -- operator classes on RUM's model. BARK takes GIN's support
  functions for extracted keys (extract a value's keys; extract a query's
  keys, with partial match; consistent) and RUM's ordering and per-locator
  data functions (config, which declares the per-locator type; ordering and
  outer ordering; the join of per-locator values), so a RUM or GIN operator
  class ports to BARK with little change. The per-locator functions need M3.
  Plus classes matching RUM's for arrays (containment, overlap, equality,
  similarity ordering) and for scalar types with RUM's distance operators,
  which BARK's KNN scan already serves on a leading column. The new
  procedures are numbered after btree's, since BARK's scalar classes live in
  btree's operator families; <code>barkvalidate</code> checks them. RUM's
  tsvector classes are out of scope: their ranking needs per-occurrence
  positions (M3) and belongs to a text-search index (see "Containment
  queries").
* '''M3''' -- extra data per locator (RUM's addInfo): a LIST or POSTING
  variant whose members carry a small value beside each TID and are ordered
  by it, so "key X ordered by a value" keeps one entry per key. A composite
  key (key, value) already does this when entries need not share; a design
  note decides whether M3 is needed.
* '''M4''' -- unique indexes over multikey keys with document-store
  semantics: unique across rows, while one row may repeat a key.
* '''T1''' -- term containment at scale; see "Containment queries" under
  C-KEYSHAPE.

== Ground rules ==

* This document is authoritative for the contracts. A commit that changes a
  contract updates this file in the same commit.
* Catalog and TOAST indexes stay on nbtree; BARK is for user indexes.
* No process-global mutable state on the BARK read/write path; annotate
  long-lived pointers per the <code>xtc</code> multithreading work.
* Every commit is independently buildable and testable (bisectable), builds
  <code>-Werror --enable-cassert</code>, and passes <code>make check</code>.
